  "game_design_document": "## Core Game Loop and Mechanics\n\n**Rail Rider 3D** is an exhilarating 3D roller coaster experience where players ride along pre-defined tracks, experiencing the thrill of speed, loops, drops, and turns. The core gameplay focuses on the visceral sensation of movement and the ability to switch perspectives for different viewing experiences.\n\n### Moment-to-Moment Gameplay\n- Player automatically moves forward along the coaster track at varying speeds\n- Speed increases on downward slopes and decreases on upward climbs\n- Player can switch between first-person (inside the cart) and third-person (behind the cart) camera views\n- Player can lean left/right to shift their weight, creating a subtle swaying effect for added immersion\n- Track features loops, corkscrews, drops, and banked turns that create dynamic camera movements\n- Speed indicators and distance markers provide feedback on progress\n\n### Progression and Engagement\n- Complete the track to finish the ride\n- Track completion time is displayed at the end\n- Replay to experience different camera angles or try to beat previous times\n- The thrill comes from the sensation of speed, the dramatic track elements, and the immersive perspective switching\n\n### Win/Lose Conditions\n- **Win**: Successfully complete the entire coaster track\n- **No Fail State**: This is an experience-focused game - players cannot lose, ensuring a stress-free thrill ride\n- **Replayability**: Driven by the desire to experience different perspectives and appreciate the track design\n\n### What Makes It Compelling\n- Immersive 3D perspective with smooth camera transitions\n- Physics-based speed changes create authentic coaster sensations\n- Dramatic track elements (loops, drops, corkscrews) provide visual spectacle\n- Camera switching adds variety and lets players choose their preferred view\n- Simple, accessible gameplay that anyone can enjoy\n\n## Starting Scope\n\nFor the initial implementation, we'll create a focused, polished prototype that captures the core thrill of the roller coaster experience:\n\n### First Iteration (Implement Now)\n- **Single Complete Track**: One well-designed coaster track featuring:\n  - A dramatic initial climb and drop\n  - 2-3 loops or corkscrews\n  - Several banked turns\n  - Varied elevation changes\n  - Total duration: 45-60 seconds\n- **Dual Camera System**: \n  - First-person view (from rider's perspective)\n  - Third-person view (following behind the cart)\n  - Smooth transitions between views\n- **Core Physics**:\n  - Speed variation based on track slope\n  - Smooth cart movement along the spline path\n  - Camera rotation matching track orientation\n- **Basic Controls**:\n  - Camera toggle button (mobile & desktop)\n  - Optional lean left/right for subtle cart tilt\n- **Visual Polish**:\n  - Skybox environment\n  - Track with supports and structure\n  - Simple but appealing cart model\n  - Speed lines or motion blur effect\n- **UI Elements**:\n  - Speed indicator\n  - Distance/progress bar\n  - Camera mode indicator\n  - Completion screen with time\n\n### Future Iterations (Plan For Later)\n- Multiple tracks with different themes (mountain, city, space)\n- Collectible items along the track (stars, coins)\n- Track obstacles that require ducking or leaning\n- Photo mode to capture moments\n- Track editor for custom coaster designs\n- Multiplayer ghost racing\n- Achievement system\n\n## Game Perspective\n\n**3D Third-Person / First-Person Hybrid**\n\nThe game uses a dynamic 3D camera system that players can switch between:\n\n### Third-Person View (Default)\n- Camera positioned 3-5 units behind and 2-3 units above the cart\n- Follows the cart smoothly with slight lag for cinematic feel\n- Shows the cart, track ahead, and surrounding environment\n- Best for appreciating the track design and spatial awareness\n- Camera rotates to match track banking and orientation\n\n### First-Person View\n- Camera positioned at rider's eye level inside the cart\n- Locked to cart rotation for maximum immersion\n- Shows hands gripping the safety bar in peripheral vision\n- Creates intense sensation of speed and height\n- Best for experiencing the thrill and drops\n\n### Camera Behavior\n- Smooth interpolation when switching between views (0.3-0.5 second transition)\n- Both cameras follow the track's orientation (banking, loops, inversions)\n- Slight camera shake at high speeds for added intensity\n- Look-ahead system keeps upcoming track visible\n\n## Game World and Level Design\n\n### Environment\nThe coaster exists in a vibrant, open sky environment with:\n- **Skybox**: Bright blue sky with scattered clouds, creating a sense of height\n- **Ground Plane**: Distant ground far below (500+ units) with simple terrain texture\n- **Atmospheric Elements**: Light fog in the distance for depth perception\n- **Lighting**: Bright directional sunlight creating clear shadows and depth\n\n### Track Design - \"Sky Serpent\"\n\nThe track is a continuous 3D spline path that creates an exciting 45-60 second ride:\n\n#### Section 1: The Climb (0-15 seconds)\n- Starts at ground level with a gentle forward movement\n- Steep 45-degree climb up to 80 units height\n- Builds anticipation as speed decreases\n- Player can see the entire track layout ahead\n\n#### Section 2: The First Drop (15-25 seconds)\n- Sharp 60-degree drop creating stomach-dropping sensation\n- Speed rapidly increases\n- Leads into a large vertical loop (20 unit radius)\n- Exit loop with high speed maintained\n\n#### Section 3: The Corkscrew (25-35 seconds)\n- Series of 2 corkscrew rotations (full 360° barrel rolls)\n- Moderate speed through twisting section\n- Disorienting but thrilling camera rotation\n- Exits into a banked turn\n\n#### Section 4: The Wave (35-45 seconds)\n- Series of 3 smaller hills creating a wave pattern\n- Speed varies with each rise and fall\n- Banked turns between hills\n- Gradual descent toward finish\n\n#### Section 5: The Finale (45-60 seconds)\n- Final moderate climb\n- Sharp banked turn (60-degree banking)\n- Gentle descent to finish line\n- Speed decreases as track levels out\n\n### Track Technical Specifications\n- **Track Width**: 2 units (enough for cart with safety margins)\n- **Cart Size**: 1.5 units wide, 1 unit tall, 2 units long\n- **Track Material**: Metallic rails with wooden or metal cross-beams\n- **Support Structure**: Visible support beams every 10-15 units\n- **Safety Rails**: Visible side rails along the entire track\n- **Total Track Length**: Approximately 400-500 units\n- **Height Range**: 0 to 80 units above ground\n\n### Track Implementation\n- Use THREE.js CatmullRomCurve3 for smooth spline path\n- Define track as series of 3D control points\n- Generate track geometry by extruding along the curve\n- Calculate tangent, normal, and binormal vectors for proper cart orientation\n- Support structure generated procedurally based on track height\n\n## Edit Mode Design\n\nEdit mode allows players to customize the visual experience and some ride parameters while maintaining the core track design:\n\n### Editable Properties\n\n#### Visual Customization\n- **Cart Color**: RGB color picker for cart body\n  - Default: Red (#FF0000)\n  - Constraint: Any valid color\n- **Track Color**: RGB color picker for track rails\n  - Default: Silver (#C0C0C0)\n  - Constraint: Any valid color\n- **Sky Theme**: Dropdown selection\n  - Options: \"Day\" (blue sky), \"Sunset\" (orange/pink), \"Night\" (dark with stars)\n  - Default: Day\n- **Environment Fog**: Toggle and color picker\n  - Default: Light blue fog, enabled\n  - Constraint: Fog density 0-100%\n\n#### Ride Parameters\n- **Starting Speed**: Slider control\n  - Range: 5-15 units/second\n  - Default: 10 units/second\n  - Constraint: Affects initial momentum only\n- **Gravity Strength**: Slider control\n  - Range: 0.8x to 1.2x normal gravity\n  - Default: 1.0x\n  - Constraint: Affects speed gain/loss on slopes\n- **Camera Smoothness**: Slider control\n  - Range: 0.1 (snappy) to 1.0 (very smooth)\n  - Default: 0.5\n  - Constraint: Affects camera follow lag\n\n#### UI Customization\n- **Show Speed Indicator**: Toggle\n  - Default: On\n- **Show Progress Bar**: Toggle\n  - Default: On\n- **Motion Blur Effect**: Toggle\n  - Default: On\n\n### Edit Mode Interface\n- Properties panel slides in from the right side\n- Live preview of changes (except ride parameters which require restart)\n- \"Reset to Defaults\" button\n- \"Start Ride\" button to exit edit mode and begin playing\n\n### Constraints and Validation\n- All color values must be valid hex or RGB\n- Numeric sliders have hard min/max limits\n- Track geometry itself is not editable (too complex for first iteration)\n- Changes are saved to window.gameConfig automatically\n\n## Control Design\n\n**Rail Rider 3D** uses a simplified control scheme appropriate for an on-rails experience where the player is automatically moving forward along a fixed path.\n\n### Mobile Controls (Touch)\n\n#### Primary Control: Camera Toggle Button\n- Large button in bottom-right corner (120x120 pixels)\n- Icon shows camera symbol with \"FP/TP\" text\n- Tap to switch between first-person and third-person views\n- Button remains visible and accessible at all times\n- Visual feedback on tap (button press animation)\n\n#### Secondary Control: Lean (Optional)\n- Touch and hold left side of screen (left 40% of screen width) to lean left\n- Touch and hold right side of screen (right 40% of screen width) to lean right\n- Creates subtle cart tilt effect (±10 degrees max)\n- Visual indicators appear when leaning (arrows or tilt indicator)\n- Center 20% of screen reserved for camera button area\n\n#### Touch Implementation Details\n- Track all touch IDs to prevent interference\n- Camera button has priority (touches in button area don't trigger lean)\n- Lean is analog based on horizontal touch position\n- Release touch to return to center\n- Multiple simultaneous touches supported (lean + camera toggle)\n\n### Desktop Controls (Keyboard + Mouse)\n\n#### Primary Controls\n- **C Key** or **V Key**: Toggle between first-person and third-person camera\n- **A Key** or **Left Arrow**: Lean left\n- **D Key** or **Right Arrow**: Lean right\n- **Space Bar**: Alternative camera toggle\n\n#### Mouse Controls\n- **Mouse Movement**: No effect (camera is locked to track)\n- **Left Click**: Toggle camera view (same as C key)\n- **Right Click**: No function\n\n#### Keyboard Implementation Details\n- Prevent default browser behavior for used keys (arrows, space)\n- Convert keycodes to lowercase before checking state\n- Clear all input states on window blur\n- Lean keys provide binary input (full lean or none)\n- Smooth interpolation applied to lean angle\n\n### Control Feedback\n\n#### Visual Feedback\n- Camera toggle button highlights on press\n- Lean indicators appear on screen edges when leaning\n- Smooth camera transition animation (0.3 seconds)\n- Cart visibly tilts when leaning\n\n#### Audio Feedback\n- Subtle \"click\" sound on camera toggle\n- Wind sound intensity increases with speed\n- Track rumble sound when at high speed\n\n### Accessibility Considerations\n- Large touch targets (minimum 96x96 pixels)\n- Camera button positioned in thumb-friendly zone (bottom-right)\n- Multiple input methods for same action (keyboard alternatives)\n- Visual indicators for all active controls\n- No time-critical inputs required (experience-focused game)\n\n### Control Simplicity\n- Only 2 actions: camera toggle and lean\n- Lean is optional (doesn't affect completion)\n- No complex combinations or timing required\n- Controls explained in brief tutorial overlay at start\n- Intuitive icons and labels for all buttons\n\n## Art Style and Visual Theme\n\n### Overall Art Direction\n**Modern Low-Poly 3D with Vibrant Colors**\n\nThe game uses a clean, modern 3D aesthetic that balances visual appeal with performance. The style is colorful and inviting, emphasizing the fun and excitement of the roller coaster experience.\n\n### Visual Style Characteristics\n- **Geometry**: Low-poly 3D models with smooth shading\n- **Colors**: Vibrant, saturated colors with good contrast\n- **Lighting**: Bright, clear lighting with soft shadows\n- **Atmosphere**: Clean and optimistic, emphasizing the thrill without being scary\n- **Detail Level**: Simple but polished - focus on silhouette and color over texture detail\n\n### Color Palette and Mood\n- **Primary Colors**: \n  - Track: Metallic silver/gray (#C0C0C0)\n  - Cart: Bright red (#FF3333) with yellow accents\n  - Sky: Clear blue (#87CEEB) with white clouds\n- **Accent Colors**:\n  - Support structures: Dark gray (#404040)\n  - UI elements: White with blue highlights\n  - Speed effects: Cyan/white motion lines\n- **Mood**: Exciting, energetic, and fun - like a theme park on a perfect sunny day\n\n### Asset Specifications\n\n#### 3D Models (Code-Rendered with THREE.js)\nAll 3D elements will be created programmatically using THREE.js geometries:\n\n- **Cart**: \n  - BoxGeometry for main body (1.5 x 1 x 2 units)\n  - CylinderGeometry for wheels (4 wheels, 0.3 unit radius)\n  - Simple seat shapes using BoxGeometry\n  - Safety bar using CylinderGeometry\n  - MeshStandardMaterial with metallic red color\n  \n- **Track**:\n  - TubeGeometry extruded along CatmullRomCurve3 spline\n  - Radius: 0.15 units for rails\n  - Cross-beams using BoxGeometry every 2 units\n  - MeshStandardMaterial with metallic silver color\n  \n- **Support Structures**:\n  - CylinderGeometry for vertical beams\n  - BoxGeometry for cross-bracing\n  - Procedurally placed based on track height\n  - MeshStandardMaterial with dark gray color\n\n#### Environment (Code-Rendered)\n- **Skybox**: THREE.js Sky shader or gradient background\n- **Ground Plane**: Large PlaneGeometry with simple texture or color\n- **Clouds**: Simple white spheres with low opacity, positioned in sky\n- **Fog**: THREE.js Fog for atmospheric depth\n\n#### Visual Effects (Code-Rendered)\n- **Motion Blur**: Post-processing effect or speed lines\n- **Speed Lines**: Particle system with white/cyan lines\n- **Camera Shake**: Procedural camera offset at high speeds\n- **Glow Effects**: Bloom post-processing for highlights\n\n#### UI Elements (CSS/Canvas)\n- **Speed Indicator**: Circular gauge rendered with Canvas 2D\n- **Progress Bar**: CSS-styled horizontal bar\n- **Camera Mode Indicator**: CSS icon with text\n- **Buttons**: CSS-styled with icons\n- **Completion Screen**: CSS overlay with stats\n\n### Generated Assets Needed\n\nOnly a few specific assets need to be generated:\n\n1. **UI Icons**: Camera toggle icon, lean indicators\n2. **Sound Effects**: Covered in audio section\n3. **Particle Textures**: Simple circular gradient for speed lines\n\n### Viewing Angles and Consistency\n- **Cart**: Modeled to look good from both behind (third-person) and inside (first-person)\n- **Track**: Consistent rail width and cross-beam spacing throughout\n- **Environment**: 360-degree consistency (player can see all directions)\n- **Lighting**: Single directional light from above-front for consistent shadows\n\n### Scale and Proportions\n- **Cart**: 1.5 units wide (comfortable single-rider size)\n- **Track**: 2 units wide (provides safety margin around cart)\n- **Human Scale**: Cart sized for average adult (1.7 units tall when standing)\n- **Environment Scale**: Track reaches 80 units high (feels impressively tall)\n- **Viewing Distance**: Third-person camera 3-5 units back (good overview)\n\n### Technical Rendering Approach\n- Use THREE.js WebGL renderer for all 3D content\n- MeshStandardMaterial for physically-based rendering\n- Simple shadow mapping for depth\n- Post-processing for motion blur and bloom\n- Maintain 60 FPS on mobile devices through optimization",
